/*!
@defgroup power_manager_ytm32b1mx Power Manager (YTM32B1Mx)
@ingroup power_manager
@brief Device-specific Power Manager implementation for the YTM32B1Mx series.

@details
This module provides the YTM32B1Mx-specific implementation of the Power Manager
driver. It implements the platform-specific POWER_SYS_Do* functions that
perform the actual hardware register manipulation for power mode transitions
on the YTM32B1M series MCUs.

@section power_b1mx_features Key Features
- Supports RUN, SLEEP, DEEPSLEEP, and STANDBY power modes (all variants).
- Supports POWERDOWN mode (on devices with FEATURE_SOC_SUPPORT_POWERDOWN_MODE).
- Supports DEEP POWERDOWN mode (on devices with FEATURE_SOC_SUPPORT_DEEPPOWERDOWN_MODE).
- CMU (Clock Monitoring Unit) save/restore around low-power entry.
- SysTick disable/restore to prevent spurious wakeup.
- FXOSC wait-for-valid after wakeup from deep sleep modes.
- Cache invalidate/restore around low-power entry (on devices with FEATURE_SOC_SUPPORT_CACHE).
- Flash idle wait before low-power entry (errata E220008 workaround).

@section power_b1mx_modes Supported Power Modes
| Power Mode     | Description                                                        |
|----------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------|
| RUN            | Normal run mode, full clock and peripherals.                       |
| SLEEP          | Core in sleep, normal interrupt wakeup, peripherals active.        |
| DEEPSLEEP      | Most clocks gated, async interrupt wakeup.                         |
| STANDBY        | Internal LDO in low power, async interrupt or WKU wakeup.         |
| POWERDOWN      | Only part of digital powered, WKU wakeup (device-dependent).      |
| DEEP POWERDOWN | Deepest low-power state, limited wakeup sources (device-dependent).|

@section power_b1mx_config Configuration
@subsection power_b1mx_config_struct power_manager_user_config_t
| Field           | Type                    | Description                                       |
|-----------------|-------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|
| powerMode       | power_manager_modes_t   | Target power mode to enter.                        |
| sleepOnExitValue| bool                    | If true, core returns to sleep after ISR completes.|

@section power_b1mx_usage Usage Example
@code{.c}
power_manager_user_config_t pwrMan_InitConfig = {
    .powerMode = POWER_MANAGER_RUN,
    .sleepOnExitValue = false,
};

power_manager_user_config_t *powerConfigsArr[] = {
    &pwrMan_InitConfig
};

power_manager_callback_user_config_t *powerCallbacksConfigsArr[] = {(void *)0};

if (STATUS_SUCCESS != POWER_SYS_Init(&powerConfigsArr, 1, &powerCallbacksConfigsArr, 0)) {
    /* Handle error */
}

if (STATUS_SUCCESS != POWER_SYS_SetMode(0, POWER_MANAGER_POLICY_AGREEMENT)) {
    /* Handle error */
}
@endcode

@section power_b1mx_integration Integration Guide
@subsection power_b1mx_files Compilation Units
The following source files must be compiled in the project:
@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/power/power_manager.c
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/power/YTM32B1Mx/power_manager_YTM32B1Mx.c
@endverbatim

@subsection power_b1mx_include Include Paths
The following paths must be added to the include path of the toolchain:
@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/inc/
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/power/
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/power/YTM32B1Mx/
@endverbatim

@subsection power_b1mx_symbols Compile Symbols
No special symbols are required for this component. Platform-specific feature
macros are automatically defined in the device features header.

@subsection power_b1mx_deps Dependencies
- @ref clock_manager — Required for FXOSC valid wait after wakeup.

@section power_b1mx_notes Important Notes

1. The Power Manager does not check if necessary wakeup sources are enabled.
   It is the responsibility of the application to configure wakeup sources
   before calling POWER_SYS_SetMode().

2. The debug module is not available under DeepSleep/Standby/PowerDown modes.
   Disconnect the debugger before low-power entry.

3. Do not call POWER_SYS_SetMode() from an interrupt service routine.

4. If the application enters low-power mode immediately at startup, the
   debugger may not be able to connect. Add a startup delay during development.

*/
